On Fri, Nov 08, 2002 at 09:50:56PM +0000, Thorsten Kranzkowski wrote: > > if you will try to _reduce_ number of casts by making sure that types of
> > objects agree, if only possible, instead of sprinkling code with extra
> 
> I've actually done that. 

You mean in a version you are working on now?  There was quite
a few extra casts in what I have seen.  Some of them appeared
"obviously correct" but this was not the case for others.

> > But if you want a unsigned constant with all bits on then ~0UL is surely
> > preferable over casting (-1) to unsigned long; even if just for clarity.
> 
> ok, what do you suggest when there's a 
> return ( x ? 0 : -ENOMEM )
> in a function declared as unsigned long ?

I noticed this in a patch for setup.c:

-       if (bootmap_start == -1) {
+       if (bootmap_start == (unsigned long)-1L) {

What I meant that it seems that you really wanted here

       if (bootmap_start == ~0UL) {

I did not check if there are other spots of that sort.

If you refer to what displeased Richard then if a return value from
arch_get_unmapped_area_1() is abused to return also an error code of
'int' type then a cast to (unsigned long), i.e. to what is returned but
not to (unsigned), on -ENOMEM  could be the only sane way out.  If
-ENOMEM is really an 'int' constant then likey you could get away with
'if ((int)addr != -ENOMEM)' but this is abusing what we know about
expected bit patters which can result from a call to
arch_get_unmapped_area_1() and I do not think that taking such risks is
a correct way to do it.

   Michal




