On Monday, November 11, 2002, at 05:59 PM, Joakim Roubert wrote:
> I've been away from the list and the alpha world for a while, but now 
> I'm
> back again. Has RH finally dropped all support for alpha?
>
> I considered switching my RH 7.1 to Debian next week or so. Are there
> other distributions that support alpha better? How does the evolution 
> look
> here?

Red Hat did 7.2 under contract to Compaq.

According to the folks in the RedHat RoadShow van just before LISA last 
week... RedHat is more than willing to do any further ports that HP (or 
anyone else) wants to pay for. However, they have very limited 
resources and won't be doing them on their own.

Put another way RedHat itself sees no market (ie zero positive income) 
and therefore no reason to deal with any architecture except x86 -- 
note that this includes the Itanium and IA64. (They did do one port to 
IA64 which HP paid for.) Similarly, they have no plans to support AMDs 
Hammer, but since it doesn't exist yet (like the Itaniaum and IA64), 
they don't consider it to be anything to be contemplating in the near 
term future.

Their attitude was quite informative and interesting. Especially since 
the "media" considers RedHat to equal Linux.

T.T.F.N.
