Fourth (and finally, for now), aboot-0.9a doesn't compile
against recent glibc versions.  The problem is that it tries to
override the printf() function, and glibc won't just go along
with that. The attached patch works around this problem, but
you probably won't be able to build aboot in "TESTING" mode
(not that you'd normally want to).

I discovered most of this over the course of the last week,
while LFS'ing my own alpha box for the first time.  Who knows
what else I'll discover?  I'm kind of auditing code as I go for
64-bit cleanliness.

--
Kelledin
"If a server crashes in a server farm and no one pings it, does
it still cost four figures to fix?"
