On Tuesday 12 November 2002 07:09 pm, you wrote:
> The GCC patch didnt help...well it did for a few minutes.
>
> First I applied the patch from LFS (Gcc 2.2.5-2 patch)
>
> Then yours.
>
> Now the error I get is
>
> :lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-build/linuxthreads
>
> /static/src/fresh/lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-build/sunrpc/rpcgen
> -Y ../scripts -c rpcsvc/bootparam_prot.x -o
> /static/src/fresh/lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-build/sunrpc/xbootpar
>am_prot.T make[2]: ***
> [/static/src/fresh/lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-build/sunrpc/xbootpa
>ram_prot.stmp] Illegal instruction
> make[2]: Leaving directory
> `/static/src/fresh/lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-2.2.5/sunrpc'
> make[1]: *** [sunrpc/others] Error 2
> make[1]: Leaving directory
> `/static/src/fresh/lfs-packages-4.0/glibc-2.2.5' make: ***
> [all] Error 2

Well...

Now you have quite a different problem.  Something's getting a 
SIGILL (illegal instruction), which usually means it got 
miscompiled--most likely an incorrect -mcpu argument?

What's your system specs, and what CFLAGS/CXXFLAGS did you use?  
Also, are you passing any special --{host,target,build} switches 
to ./configure for anything?

Did you apply all the patches to a pristine source tree, or to 
the same source tree that already broke?  At the risk of 
sounding obvious, it's best to start with freshly-untarred 
source whenever you decide to apply new patches.

-- 
Kelledin
"If a server crashes in a server farm and no one pings it, does 
it still cost four figures to fix?"




