On Wed, Nov 13, 2002 at 08:40:12PM -0800, Alan Young wrote:
> On Wed, 13 Nov 2002 16:54:54 -0700 Michal Jaegermann
> <michal@ellpspace.math.ualberta.ca> wrote:
> >
> > Partitions of IDE hard disks are a bonus. :-)
> 
> Do you mean DOS style partitions?

No, not really.  What I meant that you can use specifications like
'initrd=hda1:something' hence these are those partitions which are seen
by your firmware.  This really implies "disklabel" partitioning and
those file systems which are recognized by an aboot code (in practice
for a hard disk a subset of ext2/ext3).  From the patch I posted one can
see that there are obvious limitations (only four IDE devices and "one
digit" partitions); mostly because that is easy and you want to keep a
bootloader code rather simple.

This was done that way as basically it is "for free" on the top of other
stuff and is pretty useful for development/testing.  Adding some
extra "custom" device is not a problem.  Doing that in a full
generality becomes hairy.

  Michal




