My suggestion would be to use the "sudo" command.  Basically, this
allows a regular user to run a restricted set of commands that
require root privileges (thus, you can give the user access ONLY
to what is absolutely necessary, and deny access to everything
else).  You would add the username to the file /etc/sudoers, along 
with the commands that that user is permitted to run as root.  The 
user is prompted for his own password (to avoid problems with 
someone else running sudo from an unattended login), and then if 
everything is correct, the command is executed.  There are man 
pages for both sudo and sudoers for details on how to use the 
command and how to add the appropriate entries to /etc/sudoers,
and I would recommend reading the security notes and caveats near
the bottom of the sudo man page.

-Mike 


