On Thu, Nov 14, 2002 at 04:42:57PM -0800, steve schwartz wrote:
> 
> I am attempting to install RedHat 7.2 on my Multia.
> 
> I am trying to install using the text mode.
>>>> b dka200 fi /vmlinux.gz fl- "root=/dev/scd0 initrd=images/cd-rom.img text"

I think you should NOT be adding the "root=/dev/scd0" to the flags
argument, as the "initrd=..." defaults the root to the initial RAM
disk, IIRC. Including a "root=..." *was* the old way of doing
installs, but is no longer necessary. In fact, you should just be able
to do:

	>>> boot dka200 -fl 3

to get text mode, as there's an aboot.conf on the CD that has many
options, among them text mode, serial console, rescue mode, expert mode,
etc, etc. Try "-fl i" and then "l" (ell) to see them all.

Also, if you're having trouble with the partition table on a disk, toggle
over to VC2 once it gets far enough to start a shell there, and then you
can do:

	# dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/sda bs=512 count=100

to clear out the front of the disk (assuming /dev/sda is the target).
Then you may have to reboot, but the next time it should squawk about
no partition table found and allow you to create one...

Finally, a couple other things to remember about UDB installs:

1. TGA server will work post-install, but you need to fill in the
   "BusID" option in the Device Section of the /etc/X11/XF86Config-4 file.
   Something like:

	BusID	"PCI:0:11:0"

   if "lspci" tells you the TGA is in slot 00:0b.0 (hex to decimal :-).

2. You'll need to use the DE4X5 driver instead of the TULIP with the
   builtin NIC. Initial boot after install to "single", and edit the
  /etc/modules.conf file to replace "tulip" with "de4x5".

Good luck!

 --Jay++

