On Fri, Nov 15, 2002 at 10:36:53AM -0800, steve schwartz wrote:
> Thank you all for your help.
> I have gotten a little further but still no go.
> 
> Michal, I tried the interactive boot, it was the same as I had gotten before.
>  >>> b dka200 fi /vmlinux.gz fl- "root=/dev/scd0 initrd=images/cd-rom.img text"
> did the same as
>  >>> b dka200 -fl I

Not 'I' but 'i' and _none_ of predefined boot lines or 7.2 CD
has "root=/dev/scd0" in it so I do not know how you could get "the
same". You also do not have 'images/cd-rom.img' nor /vmlinux.gz,
'fi' should be '-fi' and 'fl-' should be '-fl'.

b dka200 -fl 3

will boot to an installation in a text mode. 'b dka200 -fl 4' will
land you in "rescue".

> upon booting "rescue" I did the command:
> dd if=/dev/zero of=/tmp/sda count=2
> It didn't seem to change anything.

Did you have /tmp/sda node?  If not then did you create it?
Writing 1024 bytes to a file in /tmp indeed is not changing very much.

> During the install it gets to the automatic partitioning point and won't do 
> it. I then get a screen that shows:

There is something more above.

> 		Start	end	size	type
> /dev/sda1	1	3	12m	Dos 16-bit
> /dev/sda2	4	960	3853m	Linux
> /dev/sad3 	961	1017	229m	swap

This is not a BSD disk label type partition table and your SRM
firmware will not recognize it.  You can still boot from a floppy.

> Any suggestions?

Try not to skip steps from what you were told already.

  Michal




