One way to get to fdisk is to start the installation process from
the CDROM and hit ALT-CTRL-F2 to get a root text console.  IIRC,
the device nodes are in /tmp/dev or something like that, rather
than the normal /dev.  So, you'd type something on the order of

        fdisk /tmp/dev/sda

If you poke around a little, you should be able to find where the
device nodes are if they aren't in /tmp/dev.

> Is this a "mount point" issue?
> I was able to install when I during the install I edited the /dev/sda2 
> partition to have a "/" mount point but then I was unable to boot after the 
> install.
>
> During the install it gets to the automatic partitioning point and won't do 
> it. I then get a screen that shows:
> 		Start	end	size	type
> /dev/sda1	1	3	12m	Dos 16-bit
> /dev/sda2	4	960	3853m	Linux
> /dev/sad3 	961	1017	229m	swap
>
> I chose /dev/sda2 to install on.
> The install then says: "Must have a / partition to install on."
> When I edited the mount point to "/"  I then got the error message:
>
> Warning: Boot partition / doesn't belong
> To a BSD disk label. SRM won't be able to
> Boot from this partition. Use a partition
> Belonging to a BSD disk label or change
> This device disk label to BSD.

I'm definitely not an expert, but in about 99% of the several
dozen times I've seen this same question asked before on this
list, the answer has been that the disk had a DOS-style partition
table rather than a BSD-style disk label.  If you search the
archives, I'm sure you will find many replies that describe how
to use the 'dd' command to wipe out an existing DOS-style
partition table in preparation to using fdisk to create a
BSD-style disk label.  When using fdisk, make sure to issue the
command to make it write a BSD-style disk label rather than a
DOS-style partition table.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
rm.riches@verizon.net




