To the list:

When I was running redhat on my pws500a/u, it would mysteriously lose time.  I 
thought it was a bad chip at first, but checked the logs and found it 
happened in the boot sequence, at the same place and for the exact same 
amount of time, every time. 

My own thought is anything that predictable had to be a software or hardware 
bug, not a symptom of incipient failure. 

On my system, Tru64 unix will whack the clock to the year 2050, where it likes 
to have it.  It keeps its own time, set it and forget it.  On the other hand, 
Linux relies on the TOY clock to set its own time, but sometime toys with 
with the clock during the boot-up.  And if you switch consoles from SRM to 
AlphaBios, it automatically adds 20 years befoer you even boot into NT or 
Windows 2000.

This would not be all that interesting, except that losing time seems to throw 
the Linux x-server off badly enough to make a machine unusable.  

Taken in context, the relationship between system clocks and operating system 
clocks can be a little problematic. I haven't a clue what to do about it, 
except to cut down on the number of OSes running on a given machine.  

It would be nice if someone would do something about it.

jn

"Thursday"

   

