> From: Ted Goodridge <tedgoodridgejr@acm.org>
> Date: Mon, 18 Nov 2002 15:05:54 -0600
>
> I have an intellimouse explorer (wireless) that works well in X, except it 
> does not recognize the 4th and 5th button.  It does have a working scroll 
> middle button.
>
> Is there a way to get these (currently non-functional) buttons programmed to a 
> function in X?
>
> If there is a doc, I couldn't find it.  Pointing me in the right direction 
> would be greatly appreciated.

What, a question not specific to Alpha hardware?  :-)

Here's a diff of what I added to my XF86Config-4 file
to handle a Logitech Trackman Marble Plus:

===================================================================
RCS file: RCS/XF86Config-4,v
retrieving revision 1.19
retrieving revision 1.20
diff -c -r1.19 -r1.20
*** XF86Config-4	2002/01/29 01:15:32	1.19
--- XF86Config-4	2002/01/29 01:23:51	1.20
***************
*** 41,48 ****
  	Identifier  "Mouse0"
  	Driver      "mouse"
  	Option      "Device" "/dev/mouse"
! 	Option      "Protocol" "PS/2"
  	Option      "Emulate3Buttons" "off"
  	Option      "ZAxisMapping" "4 5"
  EndSection
  
--- 41,49 ----
  	Identifier  "Mouse0"
  	Driver      "mouse"
  	Option      "Device" "/dev/mouse"
! 	Option      "Protocol" "IMPS/2"
  	Option      "Emulate3Buttons" "off"
+ Option "Buttons" "5"
  	Option      "ZAxisMapping" "4 5"
  EndSection
  
I found a wealth of information by searching on google.com/linux
for relevant terms.  Some of the information was in German, which
didn't do me much good, but there was some useful info out there.
The "IMPS" above might have to be different for a different make
and model of pointer control device.  Via google.com/linux, I also
found code snippets to put in one of the preferences files to make
Netscape (Mozilla, too?) recognize the scroll wheel.  Finally,
a search of the Linux documentation might have info about handling
mouse buttons 4 and 5.

Good luck.

Robert Riches
